{"name":"Neon Sunset","hex":"#ff629f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"neon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol Ne) with an atomic number of 10. The second of the noble gases, it is a colourless, odorless inert gas.\n A form or sample of the element.","A form or sample of the element."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That resembles a neon light; extremely bright; fluorescent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/neon"},{"term":"sunset","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The moment each evening when the sun disappears below the western horizon.","The changes in color of the sky before and after sunset."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To phase out."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunset"}],"entries":[{"title":"Neon","blocks":[{"text":"Neon is a chemical element; it has the symbol Ne and the atomic number 10. It is the second noble gas in the periodic table. Neon is a colorless, odorless, inert monatomic gas under standard conditions, with approximately two-thirds the density of air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Neon_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Neon_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon","wikidata":"Q654"},{"title":"Sunset","blocks":[{"text":"Sunset is the disappearance of the Sun at the end of the Sun path, below the horizon of the Earth due to its rotation. As viewed from everywhere on Earth, it is a phenomenon that happens approximately once every 24 hours, except in areas close to the poles. The equinox Sun sets due west at the moment of both the spring and autumn equinoxes. As viewed from the Northern Hemisphere, the Sun sets to the northwest in the spring and summer, and to the southwest in the autumn and winter; these seasons are reversed for the Southern Hemisphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Full_cycle_of_a_Sunset_on_the_high_plains_of_the_Mojave_Desert.jpg/330px-Full_cycle_of_a_Sunset_on_the_high_plains_of_the_Mojave_Desert.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunset","wikidata":"Q166564"},{"title":"Sunset (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color sunset is a pale tint of orange. It is a representation of the average color of clouds when the sunlight from a sunset is reflected from them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Sunset_2007-1.jpg/330px-Sunset_2007-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunset_(color)","wikidata":"Q11696368"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/1280px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neon gas-discharge lamps forming neon's element symbol","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANeTube.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/1280px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first evidence for isotopes of a stable element was provided in 1913 by experiments on neon plasma. In the bottom right corner of J. J. Thomson's photographic plate are the separate impact marks for the two isotopes neon-20 and neon-22.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiscovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/500px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/1280px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/500px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The emission spectrum of neon shows individual wavelengths of light contributing to its perceived color when heated.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVisible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Ne-water_clathrate.png/500px-Ne-water_clathrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Ne-water_clathrate.png/500px-Ne-water_clathrate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crystal structure of Ne clathrate hydrate[38]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANe-water_clathrate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg/500px-FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg/1280px-FLORIST_%28neon_sign%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=